es:** W2, W6, W7, W8\u00d72, W9\u00d72, T6, T7, T8\u00d72.  \n**Melds:** Exposed pung of T4\u00d73.  \n\nTotal tiles in hand = 11, plus meld = 3 tiles \u2192 total tiles so far = 14 when completed with a win.  \n\nCurrent hand shape:  \n- W2 (single)  \n- W6 (single)  \n- W7 (single)  \n- W8 pair  \n- W9 pair  \n- T6 (single)  \n- T7 (single)  \n- T8 pair  \n\nPossible aims:  \n1. **Seven Pairs** \u2014 currently have W88, W99, T88 \u2192 that\u2019s 3 pairs. Need 4 more pairs out of W2, W6, W7, T6, T7, plus W8/W9/T8 are already pairs. If I can turn W2/W6/W7/T6/T7 into pairs, Seven Pairs possible. That needs 4 tiles. Unlikely.  \n2. **Normal 4 melds + 1 pair** \u2014 current meld is 1 pung. Need 3 more melds + 1 pair.  \n   - Pairs already: W88, W99, T88 \u2014 pick one as final pair.  \n   - Possible sequences: W678, T678 (T678 already T6,T7,T8\u00d72 \u2014 I can make T678 chow, leaving one T8 leftover).  \n   Also: W678 needs W6,W7,W8 \u2192 possible if I draw W5.  \n   W789 possible from W7,W8,W9.  \n   But W8 and W9 already paired, so breaking pair to chow costs a pair.  \n\nActually, let\u2019s organize hand by possible chows:  \n\nW6,W7,W8 \u2192 one chow (needs break W8 pair).  \nThen remaining: W2, W8, W9\u00d72, T6, T7, T8\u00d72.  \n\nFrom leftover W8,W9\u00d72 \u2192 W789 chow possible (W8,W9,W9? no, chow requires three different consecutive numbers, so W789 needs W7,W8,W9 \u2014 but W7 used in first chow, and I only have one W7). So maybe W789 not possible.  \n\nT6,T7,T8 \u2192 chow. Leaves T8 leftover.  \n\nSo tentative plan:  \nChow1: W6,W7,W8 (if I get W8 from pair, then W8 pair broken).  \nChow2: T6,T7,T8 (uses one T8 from pair, leaves one T8).  \nRemaining after these 2 chows:  \nW2, W8 leftover, W9\u00d72, T8 leftover.  \nThat\u2019s W2 (single), W8, W9\u00d72, T8.  \n\nNeed one more chow/pung + pair.  \nPossible: W9 pung (need one more W9). Pair could be W8/W8 leftover? No, W8 leftover only 1. Pair could be T8 leftover + another T8? No, only 1 T8 leftover. Pair could be W2 pair if draw another W2.  \n\nSeems messy.  \n\nBetter to think: We have isolated tiles W2, W6, W7, T6, T7, plus pairs W88, W99, T88.  \nIf I keep W88 and W99 as pairs, then only need 2 more chows from W6,W7 and T6,T7 plus maybe W2\u2192 but W2 doesn\u2019t connect to anything except W1 or W3,W4.  \n\nSo safest discard is to reduce singles and not break pairs unless necessary.  \n\nLooking at singles: W2, W6, W7, T6, T7.  \nAmong them, W2 is worst: only connects to W1 or W3,W4, and W1/W3/W4 are still available in wall (W1=2 left, W3=4 left, W4=4 left), but W2 itself only 3 left in wall (I have 1, so 2 left).  \n\nW6 connects to W4-W5-W6 or W5-W6-W7 or W6-W7-W8 (but W7 is also single, W8 is pair).  \nSimilarly T6 connects to T4,T5,T6 or T5,T6,T7 or T6,T7,T8 (T8 is pair, T7 is single).  \n\nGiven we already have an exposed T4 pung, half-flush possible if we stick to T suits + honors? No, we have W suits too.  \n\nActually, maybe aim for **All Simples** (no terminals/honors) \u2014 all my tiles W2,W6,W7,W8,W9,T6,T7,T8 include W9 which is terminal, so discard W9 kills terminal for All Simples possibility. But also W2,W6,W7,W8,T6,T7,T8 are all simples. With W9\u00d72 gone, W8 pair becomes pair, need 3 chows: T678, W678, and one more chow from maybe W2,W3,W4 (if drawn) or T456 etc.  \n\nBut discard W9 is dangerous? W9 is 2 in hand, 2 left in wall. Others have played W9.  \n\nAlternatively discard W2 or T7.  \n\n**Safe discards**: Look at opponents\u2019 discards and melds.  \nPrev player has J2 pung and B1 pung \u2014 likely has honors/terminals. Next player has F1 pung. Across none.  \nDiscards: W1, B1, T4, etc. B4 low.  \n\nW2: next player played W2 earlier. So W2 maybe safe to discard.  \nT7: not seen discarded much.  \n\nAlso considering scoring: Prevalent Wind N (F4), Seat Wind E (F1). I have none.  \nMy exposed meld T4 pung \u2192 Pung of simples, no fan.  \n\nI need 8 fan to win. Currently:  \n- All Simples (2 fan) possible if I ditch W9.  \n- Concealed Hand (if I win by self-draw after no more calls) \u2014 but already exposed T4 pung \u2192 no.  \n- Half Flush possible? No, have W and T suits mixed.  \n- Pure Double Chow possible if I get two identical chows.  \n- Tile Hog if four of a tile without kong.  \n\nGiven W8 pair already, W9 pair, T8 pair, could aim for **Seven Pairs**: need 4 more singles to become pairs: W2,W6,W7,T6,T7 \u2192 5 singles, must draw 5 specific tiles. Unlikely.  \n\nSo normal hand: need two more chows+pair. Better to keep tiles that can connect to existing pairs: W8 connects to W6,W7; T8 connects to T6,T7. So keep W6,W7 together; keep T6,T7 together.  \n\nThus worst single is W2 (no nearby tiles in hand).  \n\n**Evaluation**:  \n1. Discard W2 \u2014 reduces singles far from other tiles.  \n2. Discard W6 \u2014 breaks W678 possibility but keeps T678.  \n3. Discard T7 \u2014 breaks T678 but keeps W678.  \n\nGiven W678 uses W8 pair (so must break pair), while T678 uses T8 pair (must break pair). But T8 pair broken leaves T8 single later for pair potential.  \n\nActually, T8 pair is easier to keep as pair because T6,T7 are singles, so T678 chow breaks T8 pair, leaving me needing to find another pair.  \n\nBetter to keep pairs intact for now and discard a single with few connections. That\u2019s W2.  \n\nAlso check wall tiles: W1(2 left), W3(4 left) \u2014 possible to chow W2,W3,W4 or W1,W2,W3 if I keep W2 and draw W1/W3, but W4\u2019s low count. W4 is 4 left but others meld none.  \n\nStill, W2 has 2 left in wall for pairing. But W6 has 3 left, T6 has 4 left, T7 4 left. So W2 least likely to complete chow soon.  \n\n**Reason**: W2 is isolated terminal-ish but not terminal for 1/9, but it connects only to W1/W3/W4, none in hand. Safest discard to maintain flexibility toward All Simples (discard W9 later) and preserve W678/T678 possibilities.  \n\n**Final choice**: Play W2.  \n\nEvaluation: W2 is the most isolated tile, keeping W6/W7 and T6/T7 preserves two potential chow groups, maintains pairs for final pair flexibility, and seems safe based on discard history.  \nReason: Maintains hand flexibility and is a relatively safe discard given it has appeared before.  \nAnswer: Play W2"}
