t.\\n- Two Terminal Chows? I have T7-T9 chow which is terminal chow (7,8,9), and W7-W9 chow is also terminal chow. So I have two terminal chows? Yes, T7-T9 and W7-W9 are both terminal chows (they end with 9, but start with 7, which is not terminal? Terminal chows are defined as chows of 1-2-3 or 7-8-9. So both are terminal chows (7-8-9). So I have two terminal chows! That's worth 1 point each, total 2 points.\\n- Edge Wait? Not applicable since I'm not waiting.\\n- Closed Wait? Not applicable.\\n- Single Wait? Not applicable.\\n- Self Drawn? Not applicable yet.\\n- No Honors? I have no honor tiles in my hand or melds? Check melds: T4, T7-T9, W6-W8, W7-W9: all suited, no honors. So No Honors pattern: worth 1 point.\\n- One Voided Suit? I have tiles in T and W suits, no B suit. So exactly two suits: T and W. That's One Voided Suit, worth 1 point.\\n- All Types? No, missing B suit and honors.\\n- Half Flush? I have only suited tiles of two suits, not one suit plus honors. So no.\\n- Mixed Straight? I have chows in T and W, but not all three suits.\\n- Reversible Tiles? Not all tiles are reversible.\\n- Chicken Hand? That's default 8 points if no other patterns, but I have some patterns.\\nSo far, I have: Two Terminal Chows (2 points), No Honors (1), One Voided Suit (1). Total 4 points. I need at least 8 points to win. So my hand is not winning yet because it scores only 4 points. Therefore, I must discard a tile and try to improve.\\n\\nBut wait, I have 5 hand tiles, and I need to discard one to get back to 13 tiles. After discard, I'll have 4 hand tiles plus 3 melds = 13 tiles, waiting for the 14th. So I need to choose which tile to discard to maximize potential to reach 8+ points.\\n\\nAlso, I should consider possible future patterns. For example, if I can change my hand to get more points. Perhaps I can aim for Full Flush? That's 24 points. Full Flush requires all tiles in one suit. Currently, my melds include T4 pung and T7-T9 chow, and W6-W8 chow, so mixed suits. To go for Full Flush, I would need to get rid of all non-W tiles, but that's nearly impossible given exposed melds. Alternatively, maybe aim for All Even Pungs? Not likely.\\n\\nMaybe I can try to form another pung or kong to get more points. For instance, if I draw another W6, I could have a kong of W6, which would give Melded Kong (1 point) and maybe Tile Hog if I keep four W6 without kong? But kong is better.\\n\\nBut given the current hand, after discard, I will have 4 hand tiles that should form a pair and part of a meld. Actually, after discard, I will have 4 hand tiles: they should be grouped as a pair and two singles, or a potential chow. Currently, my hand tiles are W6,W6,W7,W8,W9. If I discard one, I could keep: Option 1: Discard W9, keep W6 pair and W7,W8. Then I need a tile to complete a chow with W7,W8 (either W6 or W9). But W6 would give chow W6,W7,W8, but that's already exposed as a meld, and I cannot use the same tiles, but I can have another chow of same numbers if I have separate tiles? Yes, you can have multiple identical chows if you have the tiles. But here, if I draw W6, I already have two W6, so drawing a third W6 would give me a pung of W6, which is a new meld. That would give me: melds: T4 pung, T7-T9 chow, W6-W8 chow (exposed), and new W6 pung (concealed). Then pair would be from the remaining tiles? After drawing W6, hand would be W6,W6,W6 (pung) plus W7,W8? Actually, after discard W9, hand is W6,W6,W7,W8. If I draw W6, I have W6,W6,W6,W7,W8 -> I can form pung W6 (three W6), leaving W7,W8 which need a tile to form chow. Not complete. Alternatively, if I draw W9, then hand becomes W6,W6,W7,W8,W9 -> back to original, but then I need to discard again. So not good.\\n\\nOption 2: Discard W7, keep W6 pair, W8,W9. Then I need tile to form chow with W8,W9 (W7 or W10? no W10). So need W7 for chow W7,W8,W9, or need W6 for chow W6,W7,W8? Not matching. So waiting for W7.\\n\\nOption 3: Discard W8, keep W6 pair, W7,W9. Need W8 for chow W7,W8,W9, or W5