honors? They have not called many recent.\n- Across: melds B4,B5,B6 chow, W3,W4,W5 chow. They seem to be collecting simples.\nConsidering discards: W6 has been discarded by previous player (Play W6) and next player (not directly). B8 has been discarded by next player (Play B8) and previous player (not). W7 has been discarded by me (Chi W7) and others? Actually, in history, \"Chi W7\" means I claimed a W7 to complete a chow? That was my action. So W7 has been used. B8 has been discarded recently by next player (Play B8). So both W7 and B8 are relatively safe? But need to check if they are dead tiles (all 4 copies exposed). For B8: unshown B8:2, meaning total 4 copies, 2 are unseen. Melds: none have B8. Discards: In history, \"Play B8\" appears twice: by next player and by me? Actually, my history: \"Play B8\" is not there. I have \"Play B8\"? Wait, my history shows \"Play B8\" no, but \"Play B8\" is in next player's history. Also across player history has \"Play B8\"? Not. So likely 2 B8 have been discarded. So 2 left. So not completely safe but not extremely dangerous.\nW7: unshown W7:3. Melds: I have W6,W7,W8 chow exposed, so one W7 is used. Discards: In history, \"Play W7\" not seen, but \"Chi W7\" means I claimed a W7, so one W7 is used in my chow. So total 4, 1 used in my chow, 3 left unseen. So relatively safe.\nW6: unshown W6:2. Melds: I have W6,W7,W8 chow, so one W6 used. Discards: Previous player played W6, next player played W6? Actually, next player history has \"Play W6\" no. Previous player has \"Play W6\". So likely 2 W6 discarded? Actually, unshown W6:2 means 2 left unseen. So 4 total, 1 used in my chow, 1 discarded by previous player, so 2 left. So similar.\nSo safety wise, all are somewhat safe.\n\nBut I need to consider which discard keeps my hand flexible for potential high-scoring patterns. Currently, I have All Simples (2) and All Chows (2) likely, total 4. I need at least 4 more points. Possible patterns:\n- If I can get a pung of dragons or winds? But I have none.\n- Mixed Double Chow? I have two chows in different suits with same numbers? No.\n- Short Straight? Possibly if my last chow is W6,W7,W8 and I have T7,T8,T9, that's not short straight.\n- Two Terminal Chows? No.\n- Pung of Terminals or Honors? No.\n- Edge Wait/Closed Wait? If my wait is for middle tile, could be Closed Wait (1 point). For B8, not. For W7, if I discard B8 and wait for W7, W7 is middle of chow? Actually, my chow W6,W7,W8, if I need W7 to pair, that's not completing a chow; it's completing a pair. So not Closed Wait. So no extra.\n- Self-Drawn (1) would help but still short.\nSo I might not reach 8 points with current hand. Perhaps I need to change hand composition? But I only have 4 tiles, limited.\nMaybe I should consider discarding to aim for Seven Pairs? But I have exposed melds, so not.\nGiven that, I might not win, so I should play defensively. Which tile is least likely to give others a win?\nLooking at opponents' patterns: Next player has F1 pung and two chows, so they might be going for All Pungs or something. They might need winds. Previous player has J2 pung and B1 pung and terminal chow, so they might need terminals or honors. B8 is a simple tile, might be safe. W7 is also simple.\nBut note: Previous player has B1 pung, so they might be collecting B suit. B8 could be dangerous for them if they need B8 for chow? They have B1 pung, not chow. Across player has B4,B5,B6 chow, so they might be collecting B suit as well. B8 could be dangerous for across if they are waiting for B7,B8,B9? They have not shown B7,B8,B9. But they discarded B7? Across history: \"Play B7\". So they might not need B8. Next player discarded B8, so they might not need it.\nW7: Many players have chows involving W? Next player has W3,W4,W5 chow, so W7 might be safe. Previous player has no W melds.\nGiven the tile counts, B8 has 2 left, W7 has 3 left. Discarding B8 might be slightly safer because fewer left? Actually, more left means more chance someone has a pair waiting. But also, discarding a tile with fewer unseen might be more dangerous because if someone is waiting for it, they are more likely to win since fewer remain. But also, if many are unseen, it might be more likely to be drawn by me.\nI think both are similar.\n\nConsidering wait quality: Discard W6 gives wait for B8 (2 left). Discard B8 gives wait for W7 (3 left). W7 has more tiles left, so higher probability of drawing it. So discard B8 might give better chance to win (though still low scoring).\nBut I also have two W6 in hand. If I discard B8, I keep two W6, which could become a pair or part of chow. That might allow other waits? As analyzed, only W7 wait.\nIf I discard W6, I have one W6, which is part of chow, and wait for B8.\nSo probability: W7 has 3 unseen out of 123 total? Actually, total wall tiles unknown, but relative: W7 has 3 left, B8 has 2 left. So W7 is more likely to be drawn.\nAlso, if I discard B8, I keep W6,W6,W7,W8. If I draw W9, I can form chow W7,W8,W9 and pair W6? Wait, then hand: W6, W6, W7, W8, W9. Chow W7,W8,W9 uses W7,W8,W9, leaving two W6 -> pair! So actually, discard B8 also gives wait for W9? Earlier I said unshown W9:0, but check unshown tiles: W9:0. So no W9 left. So not.\nWhat about drawing W5? Then chow W5,W6,W7 uses W5,W6,W7, leaving W6 and W8 -> not pair. So not.\nThus only W7 wait.\n\nGiven scoring is low, maybe I should focus on defense. Which discard is safer? Looking at opponents' recent discards: Next player recently played B8, so they likely don't need B8. Previous player recently played W6, so they might not need W6. W7 hasn't been discarded recently by others. But I claimed W7 earlier, so it's in my meld, so maybe safe.\nI think B8 might be slightly safer because next player just discarded it, indicating they don't need it. Also, across player discarded B7, so maybe not collecting B8-9.\nThus, I'll discard B8.\n\nReason: Discarding B8 leaves a wait for W7, which has 3 unseen tiles, giving a higher probability of drawing the winning tile. Additionally, B8 appears relatively safe based on recent discards by opponents. Although the hand's scoring potential is limited, maximizing win chance is currently the priority.\n\nAnswer: Play B8"}
